8 Wikipedia integration
***********************

The file ‘spofy-wikipedia.el’ provides a command to open the Wikipedia
article for the album or musical work that is currently playing.  It
handles non-classical and classical music differently, using two
distinct lookup strategies.

   For non-classical music, spofy first attempts a precise lookup via
Wikidata's SPARQL endpoint, using the Spotify album ID (Wikidata
property P2205) to find the corresponding Wikipedia article directly.
This is the most reliable path when the album is registered in Wikidata
(~40k albums).  If the Wikidata lookup finds no match, spofy falls back
to searching the Wikipedia API using the album name and artist, then
validates that the top result is a genuine album article by checking for
the presence of ‘Template:Infobox album’.  No LLM is involved in this
path, so it is fast and deterministic.  When Spotify lists multiple
artists (e.g., composer and performer), only the first name is used in
Wikipedia searches to avoid failed lookups on combined strings.

   Artist lookups similarly try Wikidata first (property P1902, ~65k
artists) before falling back to Wikipedia search.

   For classical music, a different approach is necessary because the
Spotify album name (e.g., "Beethoven: Symphonies Nos.  5 & 7") does not
correspond to a single Wikipedia article.  Instead, spofy uses an LLM
(via ‘gptel’) to extract the underlying musical work from the track and
album metadata and return its Wikipedia article title.  For example, a
track named "Symphony No.  5 in C minor, Op.  67: I.  Allegro con brio"
resolves to the Wikipedia article "Symphony No.  5 (Beethoven)".

   Classification of tracks as classical or non-classical relies
primarily on the Spotify API's genre tags for the artist.  If any of the
artist's genres match a set of classical patterns (such as "classical",
"orchestral", "opera", or "baroque"), the track is treated as classical.
When an artist has no genre tags at all — which is common for
lesser-known classical performers — spofy falls back to heuristic
matching on the track name, looking for patterns such as opus numbers
(‘Op.’), catalog numbers (‘BWV’, ‘K.’, ‘RV’, etc.), key signatures (‘in
C minor’), movement indicators (‘I.’, ‘II.’), and tempo markings
(‘Allegro’, ‘Adagio’, etc.).

   All lookup results are cached persistently in a SQLite database
(Emacs 29+ built-in support required) so that repeated plays of the same
album or track never trigger redundant API or LLM calls.  Artist
classifications are also cached.

   The command ‘spofy-wikipedia’ is the main entry point.  It reads the
currently playing track from the player state (*note Playback
control::), classifies the artist, and dispatches to the appropriate
lookup path.  When a Wikipedia article is found, it is opened via
‘browse-url’.  The command is also available in the transient popup
(*note Transient popup::) under ‘w’.

   Wikipedia lookup actions are available through Embark (*note Embark
integration::) on tracks, albums, and artists (bound to ‘w’ in each
keymap).  The function ‘spofy-wikipedia-track’ classifies the artist and
dispatches to either the album or work lookup.  The function
‘spofy-wikipedia-album’ performs a direct album lookup.  The function
‘spofy-wikipedia-artist’ looks up the artist's Wikipedia page.  All
three receive the Embark target string and extract entity metadata from
its text properties.

